  "transcribed_text": " The effective distance depends on what must cross it.  Compare three illustrative workloads  using the same regional boundary.  First, assume an optical cable path of 1,000 kilometers  and a propagation speed of 200,000 kilometers per second.  Dividing distance by speed gives five milliseconds one way.  A return traversal adds another five  before processing, queuing, or other delays.  This is a propagation calculation,  not total application latency.  Calling the connection private doesn't shorten its path.  Next, consider data supply.  Region B has a buffer containing eight equal sized batches.  Its GPU consumer uses four batches per second,  initially matched by supply.  Supply then falls to two batches per second.  The shortfall is two batches each second.  After one second, the buffer holds six.  After two, it holds four.  After three, it holds two.  The buffer has supplied the consumer throughout the dip.  If the deficit continues, the buffer empties  after the fourth second.  During the following second, supply can provide  only two of the four demanded batches.  The consumer becomes supply limited.  These are simplified flow assumptions,  not measured production rates.  Finally, consider independent regional inference replicas.  A service can test version two in one region  while routing approved version one traffic elsewhere,  then promote the change after validation.  Its rollout and session policies  define the required consistency.  A blocking exchange, buffered supply,  and independent serving create different dependencies.  Identify those dependencies before deciding  how distance, link capacity, and regional policies  affect the workload.",
